WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.396
VO: Sickle-cell disease is one of the most prevalent&nbsp;
genetic conditions worldwide.

00:00:06.396 --> 00:00:13.634
AMBROISE WONKAM: Every single year we have at least 300,000 newborns born with&nbsp;sickle-cell disease.

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:22.400
VO: The condition can cause sudden pain, as well as long-term joint and&nbsp;
organ damage, frequent infections, and anemia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.440
JIMI OLAGHERE: You can tell a sickle-cell patient by just shaking&nbsp;
their pocket, and if you hear pills dangling in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.282
there you know this guy has chronic, chronic pain.

00:00:30.282 --> 00:00:39.048
VO: Treatments are limited and the only curative solution, a 
stem cell transplant, is not available&nbsp;to everyone with sickle cell.

00:00:39.048 --> 00:00:45.520
But new treatments are being trialled that could offer hope of a life&nbsp;
without symptoms to people with the condition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:50.185
and they work through gene editing.

00:00:50.185 --> 00:00:56.416
HAYDAR FRANGOUL: If we can&nbsp;improve the lives of patients with sickle-cell disease we are not only keeping them away from&nbsp;the hospital

00:00:56.416 --> 00:01:00.887
we are actually allowing them to go to school, to go to work, to be with their&nbsp;
families.

00:01:00.887 --> 00:01:08.534
I would not be heartbroken to see my floor have not a single patient with sickle-cell&nbsp;disease having any complications related to it.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.360
JIMI: You have to try and lead a normal life as much&nbsp;
as possible but then you have this chip on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.040
shoulder that’s sickle cell that literally affects&nbsp;
every single decision you make, every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.080
thing, like when you wake up, how you wake up,&nbsp;
what you eat for breakfast, if you can go to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.931
if you could take care of your kid.

00:01:33.931 --> 00:01:38.300
VO: Over 6&nbsp;million people are living with sickle-cell disease worldwide,

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.764
three-quarters of them in sub-Saharan&nbsp;
Africa.

00:01:41.764 --> 00:01:45.669
AMBROISE: Every single year we have at least 300,000

00:01:45.669 --> 00:01:53.600
newborns born with sickle-cell disease and&nbsp;
80 percent of these children are born in Africa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.880
VO: Around five percent of the world's population&nbsp;
carries trait genes for haemoglobin disorders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.520
but that figure rises as high&nbsp;
as 25 percent in some regions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:11.635
Two parents carrying sickle-cell trait have a one&nbsp;
in four chance of having a child with sickle-cell&nbsp;disease.

00:02:11.635 --> 00:02:17.120
JIMI: I was first diagnosed with sickle cell&nbsp;
at birth. I was fortunate enough to have parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:23.600
to bring me to the United States to do the&nbsp;
amniocentesis test, of course they both knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.200
they had the sickle-cell trait, both my parents&nbsp;
are Nigerian, uh living in Nigeria at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.560
Growing up, it was tough. Um I actually even,&nbsp;
I don't remember my first crisis but I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:41.520
a reminder. I have this little deformed, uh, finger&nbsp;
that doesn't grow as quick or didn't grow with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.200
rest of my fingers that was actually my first&nbsp;
crisis that it was so bad that it caused tissue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.709
damage and that's why that finger didn't grow like&nbsp;
the rest of them.

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:55.385
VO: Pain is the most common symptom of sickle-cell disease.

00:02:55.385 --> 00:03:04.640
As sickle cells travel&nbsp;through small blood vessels they can get stuck and clog blood flow causing what are known as pain&nbsp;crises.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:12.850
JIMI: It was almost a revolving door every week, 
in and out of the hospital, just trying to keep my&nbsp;head above water.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:22.000
AMBROISE: For the past 111 years where we know sickle-cell disease exists, we unfortunately have only&nbsp;four medications that have been approved by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:29.840
FDA and hydroxyurea is the only one that is widely&nbsp;
available, but unfortunately not always accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.000
in patients in Africa for example. So most of the&nbsp;
case patient will be treated symptomatically for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.440
pain, will be treated symptomatically for anemia.&nbsp;
will be treated symptomatically for recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.480
infection without a definitive treatment.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.480
JIMI: I've&nbsp;tried the lot.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:52.098
I did hydroxyurea, at one point I did um, frequent blood transfusions
 as well - that&nbsp;helped for a while

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:57.483
then I did hydration therapy where it wasn't blood transfusions because they&nbsp;
were worried my iron levels were getting too high

00:03:57.483 --> 00:04:01.900
so you know what we're just gonna&nbsp;
flush you with liquids every Tuesday.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:10.696
VO: Until recently the only curative treatment for&nbsp;
sickle-cell disease was a stem cell transplant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.153 --> 00:04:18.260
but this comes with its own risks and requires&nbsp;
a bone marrow donor who is a close match to&nbsp;the recipient. 

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:22.720
But now new treatments are being&nbsp;
developed that could allow anyone with sickle-cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.985
disease to lead a symptom-free life.

00:04:25.985 --> 00:04:35.360
HAYDAR: We are&nbsp;currently treating patients with sickle-cell disease and beta thalassemia using a innovative&nbsp;therapy called CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.719
VO: CRISPR-Cas9 is an enzyme that can be targeted&nbsp;
to make specific cuts in a cell's DNA.

00:04:41.719 --> 00:04:51.280
HAYDAR: And this gene editing is used to target a specific gene to&nbsp;
allow the stem cells in those patients to produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.830
high levels of fetal haemoglobin.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.180
VO: Sickle cell&nbsp;
is caused by a genetic mutation.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.493
AMBROISE: Sickle-cell disease is due to a single nucleotide change

00:05:01.493 --> 00:05:10.785
that alter the structure of haemoglobin, and haemoglobin is the protein that made&nbsp;of 70 percent of red blood cell.

00:05:10.785 --> 00:05:16.400
VO: Normally haemoglobin floats freely within a healthy&nbsp;
red blood cell transporting oxygen around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.280
the body. But in sickle-cell disease&nbsp;
the structure of haemoglobin is altered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:29.120
Now after releasing oxygen to cells, the mutated&nbsp;
haemoglobin locks together, causing the shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:35.520
of the red blood cell to change from a pliable&nbsp;
donut to a more rigid, stickier, sickle shape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:43.600
Researchers are beginning to trial using CRISPR&nbsp;
to directly edit the sickle cell mutation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.637
but Haydar Frangoul’s group are trying something&nbsp;
different.

00:05:47.637 --> 00:05:56.880
HAYDAR: Interestingly, throughout evolution, babies in utero make high amounts of fetal&nbsp;haemoglobin. Fetal haemoglobin is very efficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:03.360
in transporting oxygen. When babies are born&nbsp;
they switch from making fetal haemoglobin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:09.280
to making adult haemoglobin which is haemoglobin&nbsp;
A. Haemoglobin A in the situation of sickle cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:15.040
patients has a mutation in it. In utero babies&nbsp;
do not experience any sickle cell crisis or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.560
complications related to their disease and&nbsp;
what we know is we know that this BC11A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.962
gene is what is making that switch, think about it&nbsp;
as a switch.

00:06:24.962 --> 00:06:30.733
VO: BCL11A tells your stem cells to stop producing fetal haemoglobin.

00:06:30.733 --> 00:06:36.998
By targeting it with&nbsp;CRISPR this treatment 
switches fetal haemoglobin production back on.

00:06:36.998 --> 00:06:43.669
HAYDAR: We know for a fact that&nbsp;patients with sickle-cell disease for example who have persistent high fetal haemoglobin

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:52.800
and&nbsp;there are populations specifically in western Africa and in Saudi Arabia that persist&nbsp;to have high levels of fetal haemoglobin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:59.040
and those patients do not experience significant&nbsp;
complications related to their sickle-cell disease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.720
and some of them are completely asymptomatic.&nbsp;
So what we are trying to do is we are trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.840
make the cells make high number of fetal&nbsp;
haemoglobin and that fetal haemoglobin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.143
can stabilize the red blood cell and prevent it&nbsp;
from sickling.

00:07:14.143 --> 00:07:19.409
VO: And this trial is already showing exciting results for people like Jimi.

00:07:19.409 --> 00:07:30.000
He had his&nbsp;stem cells harvested, gene edited with CRISPR, and re-implanted back into his body where they&nbsp;started producing fetal haemoglobin.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.296
JIMI: The outcome of this new treatment for me has been a miracle.

00:07:34.296 --> 00:07:40.979
It was about a week maybe a week and change, I, 
I could feel it, instantly, I could feel the&nbsp;lack of pain,

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:47.200
I felt, woah okay there's no pain, and usually because I'm attached to the&nbsp;
bed this throughout this whole period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:52.080
usually once a day I'll buzz my nurse&nbsp;
to help me out with pain medication and she'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.720
bring something and I didn't need that anymore,&nbsp;
all of a sudden I didn't need pain medication I,&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:03.440
I wanted to get up. Um, one of the weirdest things,&nbsp;
my eyes, usually because of sickle cell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.320
break down a lot of red blood cells and then it&nbsp;
shows in your eyes with the bilirubin and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:14.480
eyes become yellow. My eyes became white. Just&nbsp;
like pure white, which was of course I love that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.640
I've always wanted white eyes but it was just&nbsp;
a shock to me, I couldn't believe it, I had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.711
keep on like "is this... is this me?"

00:08:20.711 --> 00:08:23.956
HAYDAR: The seven&nbsp;patients that received this treatment

00:08:23.956 --> 00:08:29.511
all have benefited from it and have not experienced&nbsp;
any sickle-cell related complications after the treatment.

00:08:29.511 --> 00:08:37.924
VO: This treatment can't repair damage&nbsp;that sickle-cell disease has already caused, but it could prevent new problems from even&nbsp;arising.

00:08:38.206 --> 00:08:41.167
Unfortunately it's very expensive.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:47.280
Transporting an individualized therapy like this&nbsp;
from a trial setting to where it is needed most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.847
around the world will be an enormous challenge.

00:08:50.847 --> 00:08:56.631
HAYDAR: My hope is if we show that this treatment works, and the preliminary data is very exciting,

00:08:56.631 --> 00:09:04.960
that&nbsp;I'm hoping that this therapy over time will be able to scaled up to treat patients all over the&nbsp;world, as a scientist working with this therapy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:09.120
this is my goal, is to help every patient&nbsp;
affected with sickle-cell disease in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:16.240
VO: Ambroise Wonkam is optimistic that this technology&nbsp;
could be used across the globe but he also knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:20.092
there is more to be done with the treatments we&nbsp;
already have.

00:09:20.092 --> 00:09:27.680
AMBROISE: We know things that works for now for sickle-cell disease is newborn screening and&nbsp;that newborn screening needs to be accompanied by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.120
comprehensive care embedded in specialized&nbsp;
center for sickle-cell disease. We know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:39.280
those simple public health intervention works and&nbsp;
those are the first thing to do, but it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:46.000
mean that we should not add a curative alternative&nbsp;
for sickle-cell disease. So the future of gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:54.320
editing I do think have a place in low resources&nbsp;
setting, specifically if we find a way to make them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:58.252
technologically accessible, cheaper, and safer.

00:09:58.252 --> 00:10:05.429
JIMI: I love this poem by Walt Whitman, Pioneers! O Pioneers! 
Uh, that's one of my favorite poems ever,

00:10:05.429 --> 00:10:15.000
and I just feel like a pioneer. I don't mind being the guinea pig - in fact I relish this role, so&nbsp;i'm happy that i got this opportunity.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:21.760
My thing is i just want to make sure that everyone gets this&nbsp;
opportunity as well, that's my biggest thing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:30.262
this is should be released as soon as possible and&nbsp;
it should be affordable and it should be global.

